  "result": "Summary of the change:\nThis change introduces a new linter called \"onconflict\" to the Sourcegraph codebase. The linter is designed to temporarily disallow the use of ON CONFLICT clauses that specify column names during a tenant_id transition. It includes a new Go package with an analyzer, a main function to run the linter, and Bazel build configuration.\n\nCode review and suggestions:\n\n1. dev/linters/onconflict/onconflict.go:33|n\nThe `inspectString` function is called for every string literal in the code, which could be inefficient for large codebases. Consider adding a pre-check to filter out strings that don't contain \"ON CONFLICT\" before calling `inspectString`.\n\n2. dev/linters/onconflict/onconflict.go:38|n\nThe linter uses a naive string search to find \"ON CONFLICT\" clauses, which might lead to false positives in commented code or string literals that are not actual SQL queries. Consider using a more robust SQL parsing approach or at least check if the string appears to be an SQL query before analyzing it.\n\n3. dev/linters/onconflict/onconflict.go:44|n\nThe error message for a missing DO clause is not very helpful. Consider providing more context or guidance on how to fix the issue. For example:\n```go\npass.ReportRangef(n, \"Invalid ON CONFLICT clause: missing DO clause. Please ensure the clause is complete: %q\", s)\n```\n\n4. dev/linters/onconflict/onconflict.go:59|n\nThe error message for specifying an index_column_name or index_expression is quite long and may be difficult to read. Consider breaking it into multiple lines or simplifying it. For example:\n```go\npass.ReportRangef(n, \"ON CONFLICT specifies an index_column_name or index_expression.\\nPlease update to ON CONSTRAINT for unique indexes or exclude from linting.\\nClause: %q\", strings.TrimSpace(clause))\n```\n\n5. dev/linters/onconflict/onconflict.go:1|n\nThe package lacks documentation comments explaining its purpose and usage. Consider adding a package-level comment to provide context for why this linter was created and how it should be used.",
