      "text": "Certainly! Based on the provided codebase context, I can explain code intelligence index retention policies in Sourcegraph:\n\n1. Purpose:\n   Code intelligence index retention policies determine how long precise code intelligence indexes are kept in the system. These policies help manage storage and ensure that only relevant and up-to-date indexes are maintained.\n\n2. Configuration:\n   Retention policies are part of the broader Code Intelligence Configuration Policies. They can be set up through the GraphQL API or the web interface.\n\n3. Key components:\n   - `retentionEnabled`: A boolean flag to enable or disable retention for a policy.\n   - `retentionDurationHours`: An optional integer specifying how long to keep indexes (in hours).\n   - `retainIntermediateCommits`: A boolean indicating whether to keep indexes for intermediate commits on branches.\n\n4. Behavior:\n   - When retention is enabled, indexes matching the policy will be kept according to the specified duration.\n   - For Git trees (branches), you can choose to keep indexes for either the tip of the branch or any commit on the branch.\n   - For Git commits, indexes serving data for the tip of the default branch are retained implicitly.\n\n5. UI representation:\n   The UI shows a delete clock icon to indicate that a policy affects data retention. It displays whether the policy keeps indexes for \"any commit\" or \"the tip\" of matching branches.\n\n6. Resource usage:\n   As mentioned in the documentation, retention policies directly impact resource usage, particularly disk space in Postgres. It's important to balance between keeping useful indexes and managing storage efficiently.\n\n7. Best practices:\n   While not explicitly stated in the provided context, it's generally recommended to:\n   - Set reasonable retention periods based on your development cycle and needs.\n   - Be selective about retaining intermediate commits to save space.\n   - Regularly review and adjust policies to optimize resource usage.\n\n8. Integration with auto-indexing:\n   Retention policies work alongside auto-indexing policies. While auto-indexing determines when new indexes are created, retention policies control how long these indexes are kept.\n\nBy carefully configuring retention policies, you can ensure that your Sourcegraph instance maintains useful code intelligence data while efficiently managing system resources.",
